if (netdev->xdp_zc_max_segs == 1 && (flags & XDP_USE_SG)) {
    err = -EOPNOTSUPP;
    goto err_unreg_pool;
}
/* ... proceeds to ndo_bpf without MTU check ... */
Reason:
This check only ensures that the device supports scatter-gather if requested, but does not validate whether the device’s MTU fits into the usable frame space of the UMEM chunk after accounting for headroom and tailroom. As a result, a zero-copy pool could be bound even when the chunk size (e.g., 2k) provides insufficient space for the MTU (e.g., 1500 bytes plus overhead), leading to runtime failures.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
needed = netdev->mtu + ETH_PAD_LEN;
segs = netdev->xdp_zc_max_segs;
mbuf = flags & XDP_USE_SG;
frame_size = __xsk_pool_get_rx_frame_size(pool) - xsk_pool_get_tailroom(mbuf);
frame_size = ALIGN_DOWN(frame_size, 128);
if (needed > frame_size * segs) {
    err = -EINVAL;
    goto err_unreg_pool;
}
Reason:
This new validation calculates the actual space available per frame (after subtracting headroom and tailroom) and multiplies it by the supported number of segments. It then compares this total usable space against the MTU plus standard Ethernet overhead (ETH_PAD_LEN). If the MTU is too large, the bind fails with -EINVAL, preventing a misconfigured zero-copy pool from being used and avoiding runtime errors.

